  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on digital search traces as a proxy for public attention, which may skew representation toward internet-active populations.",
      "what": "This study evaluates urban inclusivity from a disability-friendly perspective across 59 Chinese cities by analyzing public attention via Baidu Search Index data and public satisfaction through survey data. The analysis reveals significant spatial disparities, with satisfaction scoring higher in eastern and megacity regions, while public attention is more widely dispersed and strongly linked to internet penetration, education, and healthcare access.",
      "why": "It establishes that public concern for disability-inclusive infrastructure does not automatically align with perceived governance outcomes, revealing critical attention-satisfaction mismatches that can help planners target interventions in cities with high public demand but low satisfaction."
    }
